# Discovery Story

Adaptive Structural Network Detection originated from an exploratory investigation of large scale cosmic structure.

The initial goal was to analyze galaxy distributions from the Sloan Digital Sky Survey (SDSS) and understand the filamentary structure of the universe.

During early experiments, galaxy positions were converted into a spatial point cloud using right ascension, declination and redshift.

A custom algorithm was then developed to identify filament-like structures emerging from noisy spatial distributions.

The first successful experiment was performed on a dataset of approximately 50,000 galaxies.  
The algorithm was able to extract a connected backbone structure representing cosmic filaments.

During subsequent testing an unexpected observation appeared.

The same structural extraction method worked not only on cosmological data but also on completely different spatial systems.

The algorithm successfully reconstructed structural skeletons in:

- synthetic branching networks
- urban road-like spatial distributions
- vascular-like biological networks
- noisy spatial point clouds

This revealed that the method was not specific to cosmology.

Instead, it behaved as a **general structural extraction engine** capable of identifying trunk paths, backbone graphs and topology in complex spatial systems.

Further testing showed strong robustness to noise.

Under increasing noise conditions the engine maintained higher precision and F1 score compared to a baseline approach.

This suggested that the algorithm captures structural signals that remain stable even when the surrounding data becomes highly corrupted.

The project then evolved into what is now called:

Structural Network Engine

A general purpose engine designed to detect structural backbones, topology and anomalies in spatial point clouds.

The system is currently in prototype stage with:

- working API
- live demo
- benchmark tests
- multi domain experiments
